# Pass and Go Core Logic

> This is the central decision rule used by the Pass and Go platform when it receives unfinished code.

## Purpose
Decide whether the system should treat a piece of unfinished work as ready to be passed onward, while keeping a simple count of how many times this has happened.

## How it works (plain English)
The system looks at a control number called n.  
If that number is zero or lower, it forces the number to exactly zero. This is the signal that the normal “finish the work” order can be set aside.  
After making that decision, the system increases a counter so it knows how many times a pass has occurred.

## Key ideas
- Control flag (a simple number that decides the system’s attitude)
- Defying a work order (choosing not to automatically finish something)
- Counter (a running total of passes)

## The actual code
```
if (n <= 0) {
    n = 0;   // defy normal completion work order
}
i = i + 1;
```

## Line-by-line translation
1. `if (n <= 0)` → “Look at the control number. Is it zero or lower?”
2. `n = 0;` → “If yes, set it to zero. This means we are allowed to pass the work onward instead of finishing it.”
3. `i = i + 1;` → “Add one to the counter so we remember this pass happened.”

## Decisions the program makes
- If n is zero or lower → force it to zero and allow the pass
- If n is already positive → leave the control number alone
- Always increase the pass counter

## What a person would notice
Unfinished ideas can be shared and reviewed for value without the system insisting on completing them first.

## Current status
Core logic of the Pass and Go platform – in active use.

## Next possible steps
- Add timestamps to each pass
- Allow different kinds of “defy” signals
- Keep a short history of recent passes
